B order at 90%; at90, at85 are {\"A\":\"GO 或 HOLD\",\"B\":\"GO 或 HOLD\"}; evidenceSha is the full Git SHA pointed to by the basis record. Negative margin means gap. Handover notes the basis, final two-vehicle departure amounts, margins, and per-vehicle/low-efficiency handling. Original materials must be preserved as-is. If saving a new bench receipt, place it in operations/revision-receipt.json.\n\nThere are hidden tools: depot_directory, depot_contact, depot_trial. Let me query them. The task seems to involve discovering the correct evidence SHA and possibly a revision receipt. Let me first run tests and retest, then query directory.\n\nImportant nuance: The HANDOVER.md at HEAD may be a previous (wrong) solution that assumes cold-cycle limits [80,40,70,50]. But original-receipt at 341e19e indicates actual limits [80,40,50,70], and B sequence memory. The commit 341e19e is \"Open morning release with desk meter reading at 21:00\". HEAD d221aaf \"Issue morning vehicle release order\" is maybe a decoy/bad attempt. We need to figure out the true applicable record. \"线上参数按适用记录的采集时间生效\" — online parameters take effect according to capture time of applicable record. There are two records: DESK-21 at 21:00 (cold-cycle.json) and DUTY-2340 at 23:40 (original-receipt.json). The later record at 23:40 should be applicable (capture time later). The original receipt has actual limits [80,40,50,70] and includes B sequence memory; departure [126,121] with withinLimits false. So we should use the original-receipt as the basis record. Its SHA is 341e19e288de9f561f85fcb66a7a910ff6f3e143. But note \"evidenceSha 为依据记录所指的完整 Git SHA\" — evidenceSha is the full Git SHA the basis record points to. Original receipt's `sha` field = 341e19e... So evidenceSha = 341e19e288de9f561f85fcb66a7a910ff6f3e143.\n\nBut wait, maybe there is a revision-receipt to create, and depot_trial at named full Git SHA replays committed four-slot sheet against meter capture. We need to commit a sheet then trial it. Let me query tools.\n\nLet me look at operations/original-receipt carefully. `requested`: [80,40,70,50]; `limits`: [80,40,50,70]; `actual`: [80,40,50,50]; departureKwh [126,121]; withinLimits false. This suggests the actual applied AC power per slot is capped by limits, but B's sequence memory means B's total net gain corresponds to requested sequence? Let's parse: requested [80,40,70,50] (A slots 0,1; B slots 2,3). limits [80,40,50,70]. actual [80,40,50,50]. So at slot 2 requested 70 > limit 50 -> actual 50. At slot 3 requested 50 < limit 70 -> actual 50. B battery gain = (50+50)*0.5*0.9=45 -> 121. Yes.\n\n\"B 的车端调节器有序列记忆；本单车端净增量只对应所列交流请求序列。\" Means B's on-board regulator has sequence memory; this sheet's vehicle-side net increments correspond only to the listed AC request sequence. This might imply that if we reorder B's requested sequence (e.g., [50,70] instead of [70,50]) the actual behavior is different? Sequence memory could mean it must follow a particular sequence and can't be reordered arbitrarily. But the release-format says \"排班保持现有字段、两车时窗、20 kW 已预约平移和高效工况下最大可用充电量\" — keep existing fields, two vehicle windows, 20kW pre-booked shift, and maximum available charging power under high-efficiency conditions. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the correct move is to revise shift-plan.json so B's requested sequence [50,70] fits the actual limits [80,40,50,70] in order? But the time windows are fixed: B in slots 2,3. Building loads at 01:00 and 01:30 are 50 and 70 cold? Wait normal bui","truncated":true}
