minently; maybe the task expects us to remedy it. But the task is not \"make withinLimits true\"; it's \"sign a release order based on net energy\". The original receipt's withinLimits false explains why B was short (request 70 at slot2 clipped, then 50 at slot3). The new [70,70] still has withinLimits false but B is not short. So withinLimits isn't the criterion.\n\nWait, maybe the correct fix is not [70,70] but to **change the 20 kW shift target to slot2** so that the fixed limits become [80,40,70,50]? No, the 23:40 record already has limits [80,40,50,70], which is fixed by time slot, independent of our shift target. The 20kW shift is a building load action; the record's limits already reflect \"20 kW shift effective\". If we change the shift target in night.json, that would change the physical building loads and caps. But we must preserve input night.json. The shift-plan's `loadShiftKw` field says 20; there's no target field. The target is in night.json (shiftToSlot=3). So no.\n\nCould we move A's charge to free up site capacity for B? A is in slots 0,1; B in 2,3. Site limit is per slot, no cross-slot. No.\n\nCould we charge B in slot1 (before fromSlot=2)? No, B not present. If we extended B's window, forbidden (\"保持两车时窗\"). No.\n\nCould we reduce B's required? No.\n\nSo the only way to B 130 is [70,70] (request over limit to avoid aux). This is likely the intended \"sequence memory\" exploit. The task's title mentions \"冷夜\" and \"低效率处置\"; the 6 kWh aux is a cold-night battery conditioning load. By requesting high first, B's regulator doesn't run the aux heater? Seems odd but synthetic.\n\nLet's query depot_contact for [70,70] at 85% to see if it can return at85 numbers. Maybe the fixture accepts an efficiency in question? The tool's response schema might use `night.efficiency` only. We can ask in Chinese \"85% 时...\" but likely it still returns 90% receipt. Let's try a question that asks for measured results at 85%. We can use current HEAD 6f92b62. If it return